1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:06,700
A stadium stuffed with nearly 70,000 hungry football fans.

2
00:00:06,701 --> 00:00:07,800
Eagle!

3
00:00:07,801 --> 00:00:12,340
A military mess hall packed with a battalion

4
00:00:12,341 --> 00:00:13,340
of ravenous Marines.

5
00:00:13,341 --> 00:00:18,760
And a Vegas buffet filled with gamblers eager to hit

6
00:00:18,761 --> 00:00:19,760
the food jackpot.

7
00:00:19,761 --> 00:00:22,660
We probably tried every dessert.

8
00:00:22,661 --> 00:00:25,460
When you're setting a table for the multitudes,

9
00:00:25,461 --> 00:00:27,920
you'd better come armed with tons of supplies

10
00:00:27,921 --> 00:00:32,220
logistics. From stuffing a sub with four months of grub to

11
00:00:32,221 --> 00:00:37,160
feeding millions high in the sky, it's time to chow down.

12
00:00:37,161 --> 00:00:41,160
Now, Mega Meals on Modern Marvels.

13
00:00:57,920 --> 00:01:02,160
In Bangor, Washington, 160 crewmen on the deck of the USS

14
00:01:02,260 --> 00:01:05,560
Kentucky are preparing to set sail,

15
00:01:05,660 --> 00:01:08,360
and they need to pack the cramped quarters of the 18,000

16
00:01:08,460 --> 00:01:12,560
ton nuclear-powered ballistic missile submarine with enough

17
00:01:12,660 --> 00:01:15,280
food to last the entire length of their four-month mission.

18
00:01:18,140 --> 00:01:22,620
We'll be loading 90,000 pounds in dry stores, about 30,000

19
00:01:22,720 --> 00:01:25,780
pounds in frozen stores, and another 8,000 pounds in fresh

20
00:01:25,880 --> 00:01:26,680
and chill.

21
00:01:26,681 --> 00:01:29,320
are fresh fruits and vegetables.

22
00:01:29,420 --> 00:01:33,520
That's almost 130,000 pounds of food for the mission,

23
00:01:33,620 --> 00:01:36,520
or more than 750 pounds of food per sailor

24
00:01:36,620 --> 00:01:39,920
that the submarine cooks will transform into more than 76,000

25
00:01:40,020 --> 00:01:40,920
meals for the crew.

26
00:01:44,040 --> 00:01:47,500
In all, it's a $250,000 mega-meet that

27
00:01:47,600 --> 00:01:49,900
will sustain the submariners while they patrol the Pacific

28
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:55,180
Ocean, totally isolated from the outside world.

29
00:01:55,181 --> 00:01:56,680
Preparing for this loading procedure

30
00:01:56,780 --> 00:02:01,480
starts long before the food even gets to the submarine.

31
00:02:01,580 --> 00:02:04,020
We're here in our warehouse where we receive all our storage

32
00:02:04,120 --> 00:02:05,420
before we go on patrol.

33
00:02:05,520 --> 00:02:07,220
This is where we'll sort everything out

34
00:02:07,320 --> 00:02:09,700
and get everything ready to go into our food modules.

35
00:02:09,800 --> 00:02:11,560
And these are empty food modules.

36
00:02:11,660 --> 00:02:14,400
We're capable of loading approximately 5,000 pounds

37
00:02:14,500 --> 00:02:15,500
in each module.

38
00:02:15,600 --> 00:02:18,500
We put shelves in here capable of holding 225 pounds.

39
00:02:18,600 --> 00:02:20,600
And this is what we're going to be eating and living out of

40
00:02:20,700 --> 00:02:23,500
for the whole time we're underway.

41
00:02:23,501 --> 00:02:25,700
But transferring the tightly packed food modules

42
00:02:25,800 --> 00:02:29,640
onto the submarine is no easy task.

43
00:02:29,740 --> 00:02:32,000
To get the job done, the submariners

44
00:02:32,100 --> 00:02:37,440
turn to a 214-foot crane to lift the 17 modules one at a time

45
00:02:37,540 --> 00:02:39,640
and gingerly lower them down three levels

46
00:02:39,740 --> 00:02:42,160
into the bowels of the sub.

47
00:02:42,260 --> 00:02:45,060
What we do is we pull the logistics escape trunk

48
00:02:45,160 --> 00:02:47,560
off the boat, and we install the food chute

49
00:02:47,660 --> 00:02:49,200
in this box that's right here.

50
00:02:49,300 --> 00:02:52,400
And the food chute's used to guide the modules down

51
00:02:52,401 --> 00:02:56,400
safely so that we don't do any damage to the food or the boat.

52
00:02:56,500 --> 00:02:58,400
Once all the food modules are snugly tucked

53
00:02:58,500 --> 00:03:01,640
into place on the boat, military muscle takes over

54
00:03:01,740 --> 00:03:03,640
to finish off the job.

55
00:03:03,740 --> 00:03:05,140
All hands on the actual land watch.

56
00:03:05,240 --> 00:03:06,640
Mustard topside to handle stores.

57
00:03:09,760 --> 00:03:11,760
The hand-over-hand loadout of the goods that

58
00:03:11,860 --> 00:03:14,760
don't fit on the modules guarantees the submarine will

59
00:03:14,860 --> 00:03:17,260
be filled to the brim before leaving for patrol.

60
00:03:17,261 --> 00:03:22,260
for patrol.

61
00:03:22,360 --> 00:03:23,500
This is our storeroom.

62
00:03:23,600 --> 00:03:25,860
We are fully loaded and ready to go to sea.

63
00:03:25,960 --> 00:03:28,500
As you can see, we've got a lot of food packed in between the

64
00:03:28,600 --> 00:03:30,500
mods and on top of the mods.

65
00:03:30,600 --> 00:03:34,180
We utilize all the space that we can because this is the limiting

66
00:03:34,280 --> 00:03:36,640
factor as to how long we can stay out at sea.

67
00:03:36,740 --> 00:03:39,280
We make our own water and we make our own air, but food is

68
00:03:39,380 --> 00:03:41,860
the only thing that we can actually run out of.

69
00:03:41,960 --> 00:03:45,760
The typical menu during patrol consists of a 21-day cycle

70
00:03:45,761 --> 00:03:48,760
That means the Submariners won't have the same meal twice

71
00:03:48,860 --> 00:03:51,760
within a three-week period.

72
00:03:51,860 --> 00:03:54,160
But there is one big drawback.

73
00:03:54,260 --> 00:03:57,040
Once we get underway, we only have about two and a half

74
00:03:57,140 --> 00:03:59,080
to three weeks of actually fresh produce.

75
00:03:59,180 --> 00:04:01,880
And then from there, we're eating out of canned stores

76
00:04:01,980 --> 00:04:03,440
or we make powdered milk.

77
00:04:03,540 --> 00:04:05,480
It's not as good as the real or the fresh stuff,

78
00:04:05,580 --> 00:04:06,880
but it works.

79
00:04:06,980 --> 00:04:09,520
Still, the 160-man crew can look forward

80
00:04:09,620 --> 00:04:12,380
to everything from baked lobster tail

81
00:04:12,381 --> 00:04:16,380
to shrimp jambalaya, to prime rib roasts,

82
00:04:16,480 --> 00:04:19,120
and even decadent pastries.

83
00:04:19,220 --> 00:04:22,060
It's a reward for the hazardous duty and cramped lifestyle

84
00:04:22,160 --> 00:04:25,100
they must endure for months at sea.

85
00:04:25,200 --> 00:04:26,220
Here you go.

86
00:04:26,320 --> 00:04:27,600
Thank you very much.

87
00:04:27,700 --> 00:04:29,900
The kitchen's in operation 24 hours a day.

88
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:32,860
We'll cook in breakfast, lunch, dinner, and a mid-rats meal.

89
00:04:32,960 --> 00:04:36,040
A mid-rats meal is usually consisted of leftovers.

90
00:04:36,140 --> 00:04:37,640
This is where everything happens.

91
00:04:37,740 --> 00:04:42,380
The galley and the crew's mess is their home away from home.

92
00:04:42,381 --> 00:04:44,820
Well, food's morale, period, right?

93
00:04:44,920 --> 00:04:46,920
I mean, the morale of the ship is here on Cruise Mess.

94
00:04:47,020 --> 00:04:48,780
We have never had to worry about our meals.

95
00:04:48,880 --> 00:04:50,820
So we come down, we get fed fine food.

96
00:04:50,920 --> 00:04:54,220
I mean, today's soup was a Creole soup with shrimp in it.

97
00:04:54,320 --> 00:04:55,820
Fantastic.

98
00:04:55,920 --> 00:04:58,760
Submarine cuisine has ranked as the military's

99
00:04:58,860 --> 00:05:03,200
finest dining experience since the 1940s.

100
00:05:03,300 --> 00:05:05,840
That's when sailors accustomed to more pedestrian fare

101
00:05:05,940 --> 00:05:08,100
on surface ships started getting a whiff

102
00:05:08,200 --> 00:05:10,700
of what they were missing.

103
00:05:10,701 --> 00:05:12,360
We have the best chefs.

104
00:05:12,460 --> 00:05:13,900
We have the best food.

105
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:15,900
And we just make everything happen the way it's

106
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:17,200
supposed to happen.

107
00:05:17,300 --> 00:05:18,980
We are prepping for our next meal, which

108
00:05:19,080 --> 00:05:20,340
happens to be dinner.

109
00:05:20,440 --> 00:05:22,140
We've got fish in the oven.

110
00:05:22,240 --> 00:05:24,540
We're working on braised beef and noodles.

111
00:05:24,640 --> 00:05:26,520
We got French bread ready to go in the oven.

112
00:05:26,620 --> 00:05:28,860
We make our bread from scratch on board.

113
00:05:28,960 --> 00:05:31,360
We're underway, all our hamburger buns, our hot dog

114
00:05:31,460 --> 00:05:36,360
buns, our loaf bread, everything is made from scratch.

115
00:05:36,460 --> 00:05:40,700
The crewmen not only have to eat, but drink, too.

116
00:05:40,701 --> 00:05:42,160
And since there's not enough space

117
00:05:42,260 --> 00:05:44,940
to store a four-month water supply,

118
00:05:45,040 --> 00:05:47,340
the submarine comes equipped with a water distillation

119
00:05:47,440 --> 00:05:50,340
apparatus.

120
00:05:50,440 --> 00:05:53,240
It works by taking in the surrounding seawater

121
00:05:53,340 --> 00:05:56,680
and heating it to water vapor.

122
00:05:56,780 --> 00:05:59,760
This separates and removes the salts.

123
00:05:59,860 --> 00:06:02,520
Then the device cools the vapor until it condenses

124
00:06:02,620 --> 00:06:05,920
into a collecting tank of fresh water.

125
00:06:06,020 --> 00:06:08,900
The distillation equipment produces about 12,000 gallons

126
00:06:08,901 --> 00:06:12,140
of fresh water per day for drinking, cooking, and cleaning.

127
00:06:15,240 --> 00:06:17,300
But all the equipment and weaponry aboard

128
00:06:17,400 --> 00:06:20,840
leaves little space for living.

129
00:06:20,940 --> 00:06:24,880
On this submarine that's 560 feet long, 42 feet wide,

130
00:06:24,980 --> 00:06:27,580
and about four stories tall, they

131
00:06:27,680 --> 00:06:31,760
leave just 300 square feet for the galley.

132
00:06:31,860 --> 00:06:34,220
And when it's time to chow down, there's only enough space

133
00:06:34,221 --> 00:06:40,360
to feed about 45 sailors, or one third of the crew at a time.

134
00:06:40,460 --> 00:06:44,060
But the Navy has tailored a solution to meet the challenge.

135
00:06:44,160 --> 00:06:47,160
We'll wake a guy up at half an hour before he is to eat.

136
00:06:47,260 --> 00:06:49,260
He comes in a cruise mess, gets his meal, he sits down,

137
00:06:49,360 --> 00:06:51,580
he has 10 to 15 minutes to eat his food.

138
00:06:51,680 --> 00:06:53,480
And then he's got to clear his plates away

139
00:06:53,580 --> 00:06:55,980
and go relieve his watch wherever he is on the boat.

140
00:06:56,080 --> 00:06:58,880
The person he changes shift with then comes down here,

141
00:06:58,980 --> 00:07:00,480
and he goes through the same thing.

142
00:07:00,580 --> 00:07:04,220
And all this happens within a one hour time frame.

143
00:07:04,221 --> 00:07:06,520
The only thing more challenging than preparing and eating

144
00:07:06,521 --> 00:07:10,920
the sub's mega meals is the cleanup afterward.

145
00:07:10,921 --> 00:07:13,360
There simply isn't enough space to collect and store food

146
00:07:13,361 --> 00:07:15,560
scraps and garbage.

147
00:07:15,561 --> 00:07:17,860
So crewmen must compact the trash daily

148
00:07:17,861 --> 00:07:23,180
into steel mesh cans and dispose them at sea.

149
00:07:23,181 --> 00:07:25,280
This is our trash disposal room.

150
00:07:25,281 --> 00:07:26,680
We roll our own cans underway.

151
00:07:26,681 --> 00:07:28,180
Because we can't bring roll cans,

152
00:07:28,181 --> 00:07:30,420
we don't have the space for it.

153
00:07:30,421 --> 00:07:32,040
After rolling the can, the crewman

154
00:07:32,041 --> 00:07:36,040
places it below a custom-fit trash compactor.

155
00:07:36,041 --> 00:07:39,340
Then we'll take our trash and set it into the compactor.

156
00:07:39,341 --> 00:07:41,440
Then we'll use 3,000 pounds of hydraulics

157
00:07:41,441 --> 00:07:44,940
to force this ram down and compact our trash.

158
00:07:44,941 --> 00:07:48,540
We'll make sure it meets the right weight,

159
00:07:48,541 --> 00:07:50,440
put the lid on it, and then we'll come over here

160
00:07:50,441 --> 00:07:52,540
to the trash disposal unit.

161
00:07:52,541 --> 00:07:55,640
We'll load four cans into it, and we'll use 10,000 pounds

162
00:07:55,641 --> 00:07:57,540
of water to flush it out.

163
00:07:57,541 --> 00:07:59,040
The holes in the can allow water

164
00:07:59,041 --> 00:08:01,980
to seep into the compacted trash.

165
00:08:02,080 --> 00:08:05,180
This eliminates any buoyancy and ensures the can sinks

166
00:08:05,280 --> 00:08:07,480
to its final resting place at the bottom of the ocean.

167
00:08:10,520 --> 00:08:13,980
Plastics are the only waste not disposed at sea.

168
00:08:14,080 --> 00:08:18,020
They're kept on board until the sub returns to port.

169
00:08:18,120 --> 00:08:19,960
Until that time arrives, the crew

170
00:08:20,060 --> 00:08:23,940
relies on food for more than just comfort and nourishment.

171
00:08:24,040 --> 00:08:25,640
On a submarine, we tend to lose track of time.

172
00:08:25,740 --> 00:08:26,740
We don't get any sunlight.

173
00:08:26,840 --> 00:08:28,640
So the way we can tell what time it is during the day

174
00:08:28,641 --> 00:08:31,040
is usually by the meal that's being served.

175
00:08:31,140 --> 00:08:33,240
If it's 5.30, we don't know if it's 5.30 a.m.

176
00:08:33,340 --> 00:08:34,540
or if it's 5.30 p.m.

177
00:08:34,640 --> 00:08:35,840
So you'll come into the crew's mess

178
00:08:35,940 --> 00:08:37,740
and you'll look at the food that's being served.

179
00:08:37,840 --> 00:08:42,080
And immediately, if you have a food identifier on the table,

180
00:08:42,180 --> 00:08:43,980
such as A1 steak sauce, then you'll

181
00:08:44,080 --> 00:08:45,180
know that you're being served steak.

182
00:08:45,280 --> 00:08:47,600
That means it's probably dinner time.

183
00:08:47,700 --> 00:08:50,100
While this dinner helps members of the silent service

184
00:08:50,200 --> 00:08:55,140
keep track of time hundreds of feet below the ocean surface,

185
00:08:55,240 --> 00:08:58,000
the military mega-meal being prepared here

186
00:08:58,001 --> 00:09:03,000
reward after a hard month of live combat training in the desert.

187
00:09:03,100 --> 00:09:07,000
15 cooks at the Marine Corps Air Ground Combat Center in 29

188
00:09:07,100 --> 00:09:11,240
Palms, California are busily prepping a Mexican feast called

189
00:09:11,340 --> 00:09:16,180
the Victory Meal for more than 1,000 hungry Marines.

190
00:09:16,280 --> 00:09:22,760
On the menu for this mega meal is 1,800 pounds of chicken,

191
00:09:22,761 --> 00:09:28,260
of chicken, 1,600 pounds of taco meat, and 4,000 tortilla

192
00:09:28,261 --> 00:09:34,180
shells, not to mention 1,900 pounds of Mexican corn, 1,300

193
00:09:34,181 --> 00:09:40,520
pounds of burritos, 2,200 pounds of baked potatoes, 2,000 pounds

194
00:09:40,521 --> 00:09:44,340
of chili, and 1,000 pounds of cheese sauce.

195
00:09:44,341 --> 00:09:46,620
We feed a mass number of Marines daily here,

196
00:09:46,621 --> 00:09:48,820
and it's a good training exercise for us,

197
00:09:48,821 --> 00:09:52,820
and it prepares us for when we have to go to battle.

198
00:09:52,920 --> 00:09:55,460
Working together as a well-oiled machine,

199
00:09:55,560 --> 00:09:58,560
each cook and the crew has his own responsibility

200
00:09:58,660 --> 00:10:02,160
in order to get the hot meal ready to eat by 1,600 hours,

201
00:10:02,260 --> 00:10:04,160
or 4 o'clock.

202
00:10:04,260 --> 00:10:06,980
And under no circumstances will we stop or make up

203
00:10:07,080 --> 00:10:09,080
any excuses for not completing the mission.

204
00:10:09,180 --> 00:10:10,580
And our mission in this particular operation

205
00:10:10,680 --> 00:10:13,580
is to get this victory meal and prep them ready to go.

206
00:10:13,680 --> 00:10:15,280
Once all the chow is ready, it's

207
00:10:15,380 --> 00:10:18,820
transported to the mess hall in rolling hot boxes.

208
00:10:18,821 --> 00:10:23,820
and set out on two buffet lines for serving.

209
00:10:23,920 --> 00:10:26,560
When the clock strikes 4, the hungry Marines line up

210
00:10:26,660 --> 00:10:27,560
by the hundreds.

211
00:10:30,660 --> 00:10:33,460
With all the running, the lifting,

212
00:10:33,560 --> 00:10:35,140
and the jumping and the training,

213
00:10:35,240 --> 00:10:36,880
we have to eat a lot of chow.

214
00:10:36,980 --> 00:10:40,000
So the food is like fuel for our bodies.

215
00:10:40,100 --> 00:10:42,880
But before fueling up on Mexican grub,

216
00:10:42,980 --> 00:10:45,340
the Marines must unload their weapons

217
00:10:45,440 --> 00:10:48,820
and, of course, wash up.

218
00:10:48,821 --> 00:10:55,820
Then it's time to pile on the nachos and celebrate

219
00:10:55,920 --> 00:10:57,020
with their brothers in arms.

220
00:10:57,120 --> 00:10:58,960
It's a great morale booster.

221
00:10:59,060 --> 00:11:00,660
It brings up everybody's spirits,

222
00:11:00,760 --> 00:11:02,960
makes us want to get out there and actually work even

223
00:11:03,060 --> 00:11:04,360
that much harder.

224
00:11:04,460 --> 00:11:06,640
Still, gathering an entire battalion together

225
00:11:06,740 --> 00:11:09,880
for a meal is a rarity, especially in the battlefield,

226
00:11:09,980 --> 00:11:13,620
where organized food facilities aren't available.

227
00:11:13,720 --> 00:11:15,420
Once deployed, these Marines will have

228
00:11:15,421 --> 00:11:19,660
Once deployed, these Marines will have to rely on a different

229
00:11:19,760 --> 00:11:25,660
mega-meal, MREs, or Meals Ready to Eat.

230
00:11:25,760 --> 00:11:31,660
Each year, the military feeds more than 35 million MREs to

231
00:11:31,760 --> 00:11:33,080
its soldiers in the field.

232
00:11:33,180 --> 00:11:39,680
The lightweight, high-calorie meals come in 24 varieties, from

233
00:11:39,780 --> 00:11:42,520
pork ribs to chili macaroni to veggie burgers.

234
00:11:42,521 --> 00:11:47,520
The tri-laminate thermal packaging preserves the meal

235
00:11:47,620 --> 00:11:49,020
similar to canning.

236
00:11:49,120 --> 00:11:51,760
The airtight pouch protects the food from oxygen

237
00:11:51,860 --> 00:11:54,600
and moisture to prevent spoilage,

238
00:11:54,700 --> 00:11:58,040
giving it a shelf life of at least three years.

239
00:11:58,140 --> 00:12:00,040
MREs are great for traveling.

240
00:12:00,140 --> 00:12:04,880
They're small, and you always have smaller snacks.

241
00:12:04,980 --> 00:12:08,120
I love when I get the Skittles or the M&Ms.

242
00:12:08,220 --> 00:12:09,620
It always makes me smile.

243
00:12:12,520 --> 00:12:16,160
Though MREs have been used in the military since 1980,

244
00:12:16,260 --> 00:12:19,560
they received a big makeover in 1993

245
00:12:19,660 --> 00:12:24,160
with the addition of a flameless ration heater, or FRH,

246
00:12:24,260 --> 00:12:28,160
allowing soldiers to heat their meals in the field.

247
00:12:28,260 --> 00:12:30,080
The way you utilize this heating element,

248
00:12:30,180 --> 00:12:33,880
what you do is basically open it up, unfold it,

249
00:12:33,980 --> 00:12:38,180
put it up from the top, grab your meal.

250
00:12:38,280 --> 00:12:39,080
The meal is beef stew.

251
00:12:42,520 --> 00:12:46,760
You wouldn't discard the box, you still need the box later on.

252
00:12:46,761 --> 00:12:49,220
Place the meal inside the bag itself.

253
00:12:49,221 --> 00:12:50,820
Take a source of water.

254
00:12:50,821 --> 00:12:52,860
And all you need to do is fill up to the line.

255
00:12:52,861 --> 00:12:53,760
And that's all it takes.

256
00:13:00,280 --> 00:13:01,280
Just watching you start.

257
00:13:01,281 --> 00:13:03,080
See the activation already going on it.

258
00:13:03,081 --> 00:13:05,880
You see the heat coming out of it.

259
00:13:05,881 --> 00:13:07,880
The flameless ration heater works similarly

260
00:13:07,881 --> 00:13:10,280
to rusting metal.

261
00:13:10,281 --> 00:13:13,520
Each FRH pouch contains a metal compound of magnesium

262
00:13:13,521 --> 00:13:16,180
and iron dust.

263
00:13:16,181 --> 00:13:19,320
When a soldier adds just one ounce of water to the compound,

264
00:13:19,321 --> 00:13:22,960
it triggers an exothermic reaction that oxidizes the metal

265
00:13:22,961 --> 00:13:23,860
and generates heat.

266
00:13:28,260 --> 00:13:31,040
Just fold it, put it back inside,

267
00:13:31,041 --> 00:13:34,000
just lean it on a rock, let it just heat up by itself.

268
00:13:34,001 --> 00:13:36,800
Takes about a minute or two, and your meal should be hot

269
00:13:36,801 --> 00:13:38,980
and ready to eat.

270
00:13:38,981 --> 00:13:42,820
While feeding an army takes some real military precision,

