510
00:26:13,700 --> 00:26:15,100
It's supposed to be a 20-pound case.

511
00:26:15,200 --> 00:26:17,480
I want to make sure that I'm not getting 18 pounds of crab

512
00:26:17,580 --> 00:26:19,940
and two pounds of ice.

513
00:26:20,040 --> 00:26:23,080
Once all the incoming food is given the thumbs up,

514
00:26:23,180 --> 00:26:26,980
it heads to the busy buffet kitchen.

515
00:26:27,080 --> 00:26:29,920
It equals about a ton of product a day

516
00:26:30,020 --> 00:26:31,320
that we actually cook.

517
00:26:31,321 --> 00:26:35,060
We have about 150 cooks specifically for the buffet,

518
00:26:35,160 --> 00:26:37,460
and there's just endless amounts of equipment.

519
00:26:37,560 --> 00:26:39,800
The biggest concern would be that we ran out of food,

520
00:26:39,900 --> 00:26:41,500
which is definitely something you never

521
00:26:41,600 --> 00:26:43,500
want to do on a buffet.

522
00:26:43,600 --> 00:26:46,840
The buffet dates back to 17th century France,

523
00:26:46,940 --> 00:26:50,380
when aristocrats would display their gold and silver plates

524
00:26:50,480 --> 00:26:53,980
and rich textiles on a table called a sideboard.

525
00:26:54,080 --> 00:26:56,680
The term buffet originally referred not to food,

526
00:26:56,780 --> 00:26:58,680
but to the fine flatware and the furniture

527
00:26:58,780 --> 00:27:01,320
on which it was displayed.

528
00:27:01,321 --> 00:27:04,720
The buffet spread through Europe and then ultimately made

529
00:27:04,820 --> 00:27:07,720
its way to America. But it wasn't until it hit the

530
00:27:07,820 --> 00:27:10,720
Las Vegas Strip that it first took on the form of all you can

531
00:27:10,820 --> 00:27:13,760
eat. It started at the El Rancho

532
00:27:13,860 --> 00:27:18,280
Vegas Casino in 1946, when a local publicist set up tables of

533
00:27:18,380 --> 00:27:22,240
cold food in the casino for the late night gamblers to enjoy,

534
00:27:22,340 --> 00:27:26,280
for just $1. And it was basically designed

535
00:27:26,380 --> 00:27:30,380
to lure casino guests to stay at that hotel versus the

536
00:27:30,381 --> 00:27:34,820
hotel versus the other hotels that just had coffee shops.

537
00:27:34,920 --> 00:27:37,380
Stuffing your face for a buck made the so-called

538
00:27:37,480 --> 00:27:40,820
buckaroo buffet an instant hit.

539
00:27:40,920 --> 00:27:44,720
Soon every casino on the strip followed suit.

540
00:27:44,820 --> 00:27:48,240
The average Vegas buffet costs $20 today,

541
00:27:48,340 --> 00:27:51,740
but you can still get a lot of bang for your bucks.

542
00:27:51,840 --> 00:27:53,740
Guests here love live action stations.

543
00:27:53,840 --> 00:27:56,880
It gives them a fresh meal that they can walk up and order

544
00:27:56,980 --> 00:27:57,840
themselves.

545
00:27:57,940 --> 00:27:59,380
Most buffets just have one or two.

546
00:27:59,381 --> 00:28:01,780
we've literally got 10 different live action stations

547
00:28:01,880 --> 00:28:03,380
throughout all of our meal periods.

548
00:28:03,480 --> 00:28:06,620
This is our egg and omelet station cooked to order.

549
00:28:06,720 --> 00:28:09,020
We will cater to any guest's desire.

550
00:28:09,120 --> 00:28:11,120
If somebody wants sauteed hummingbird tongues,

551
00:28:11,220 --> 00:28:13,320
we'll find it just to take care of that guest.

552
00:28:13,420 --> 00:28:14,720
There you go.

553
00:28:14,820 --> 00:28:16,940
The endless tempting choices usually

554
00:28:17,040 --> 00:28:20,840
lure diners into dishing up more than they can stomach.

555
00:28:20,940 --> 00:28:22,640
And that leaves Planet Hollywood with a ton

556
00:28:22,740 --> 00:28:26,980
of daily leftovers, literally.

557
00:28:26,981 --> 00:28:31,660
We recycle between 2,500 to 3,000 pounds of food a day,

558
00:28:31,760 --> 00:28:34,860
which translates to a little over a million pounds of food

559
00:28:34,960 --> 00:28:38,160
on a yearly basis.

560
00:28:38,260 --> 00:28:41,720
Trucks transport every scrap of that 1 million pounds,

561
00:28:41,820 --> 00:28:45,400
plus the leftovers of 11 other major casinos in town,

562
00:28:45,500 --> 00:28:49,140
15 miles down the road to RC Farms.

563
00:28:49,240 --> 00:28:53,080
Here, since 1963, owner Bob Combs

564
00:28:53,180 --> 00:28:56,980
has converted it to a different kind of mega meal.

565
00:28:56,981 --> 00:29:00,980
slop for his 3,000 pigs.

566
00:29:01,080 --> 00:29:06,120
I was raised up, my mother's preaching all the time,

567
00:29:06,220 --> 00:29:08,420
is to waste not and want not.

568
00:29:08,520 --> 00:29:13,120
When we're at full capacity, we recycle 1,000 tons a month

569
00:29:13,220 --> 00:29:15,940
of food scraps.

570
00:29:16,040 --> 00:29:18,440
It may not look very appetizing to us,

571
00:29:18,540 --> 00:29:20,240
but for the pigs, it's a gourmet feast.

572
00:29:23,340 --> 00:29:26,980
But before this 12-ton mega meal hits the trough,

573
00:29:26,981 --> 00:29:29,420
it needs to be sorted and disinfected.

574
00:29:32,320 --> 00:29:34,080
What we're doing here is we're sorting the food,

575
00:29:34,180 --> 00:29:37,860
basically looking for any watermelon rinds,

576
00:29:37,960 --> 00:29:40,860
tops of the pineapple stuff that the pigs don't care for as much.

577
00:29:40,960 --> 00:29:44,500
They like the overall garbage, the steaks, the caviar.

578
00:29:44,600 --> 00:29:46,800
They like the junk food.

579
00:29:46,900 --> 00:29:48,800
Once sorted, the soggy chunks continue

580
00:29:48,900 --> 00:29:53,300
up a 55-foot conveyor belt to the cooking pot.

581
00:29:53,400 --> 00:29:55,700
Once it gets into the pot, we have live steam,

582
00:29:55,701 --> 00:29:57,400
which is created from our boiler.

583
00:29:57,500 --> 00:29:58,900
We also have an agitator in there

584
00:29:59,000 --> 00:30:01,240
that moves the food constantly, like you

585
00:30:01,340 --> 00:30:02,540
would cook a pot of spaghetti.

586
00:30:02,640 --> 00:30:03,940
So that way nothing's sticking and everything's

587
00:30:04,040 --> 00:30:05,940
cooking fully.

588
00:30:06,040 --> 00:30:08,040
A pipe carries the steam from the boiler

589
00:30:08,140 --> 00:30:11,080
to the base of the cooking pot, where it divides into eight

590
00:30:11,180 --> 00:30:13,960
3 1⁄4-inch jets.

591
00:30:14,060 --> 00:30:17,560
The hot steam brings the slop to a boil from the bottom up,

592
00:30:17,660 --> 00:30:23,200
while the agitator mixes the mush at one revolution per minute.

593
00:30:23,300 --> 00:30:25,200
The slop cooks for half an hour, which

594
00:30:25,201 --> 00:30:27,440
off all the bacteria.

595
00:30:27,540 --> 00:30:30,340
Then it's off to the trough, where the pigs go hog wild.

596
00:30:37,440 --> 00:30:40,440
Each pig eats up to 30 pounds of the five-star slop

597
00:30:40,540 --> 00:30:45,860
each day, gaining as much as 280 pounds over a seven-month

598
00:30:45,960 --> 00:30:49,360
period before being shipped to a slaughterhouse in the Midwest.

599
00:30:49,361 --> 00:30:50,260
in the Midwest.

600
00:30:54,760 --> 00:30:56,840
I try not to get attached to too many of them

601
00:30:56,940 --> 00:30:58,500
because I know where they end up.

602
00:30:58,600 --> 00:30:59,700
I like to eat the pigs.

603
00:30:59,800 --> 00:31:01,500
I love them, but I like to eat them.

604
00:31:04,800 --> 00:31:05,940
And who knows?

605
00:31:06,040 --> 00:31:08,420
These pigs may even wind up back in Vegas

606
00:31:08,520 --> 00:31:09,620
as bacon at the buffet.

607
00:31:12,920 --> 00:31:15,180
The people running a national network dedicated

608
00:31:15,280 --> 00:31:19,360
to feeding the needy don't believe in wasting food either.

609
00:31:19,361 --> 00:31:22,120
And it's up to them to secure more than 70 tons of food

610
00:31:22,121 --> 00:31:25,440
every day to feed the hungry in the largest city

611
00:31:25,441 --> 00:31:26,540
in the United States.

612
00:31:29,880 --> 00:31:33,600
On March 28, 2006, the Las Vegas Hilton

613
00:31:33,601 --> 00:31:37,640
served the world's largest buffet to more than 850 people.

614
00:31:38,320 --> 00:31:42,340
The spread contained 510 dishes, including 40 soups,

615
00:31:42,341 --> 00:31:45,180
100 salads, and 150 desserts.

616
00:31:49,360 --> 00:31:55,360
It's 5.30 a.m. in New York, and this 90,000 square foot

617
00:31:55,361 --> 00:31:59,360
warehouse in the Bronx is already bustling with activity.

618
00:31:59,361 --> 00:32:04,360
In the next hour, workers here will ship out more than 150,000

619
00:32:04,361 --> 00:32:09,360
pounds of food to about 1,000 nonprofit food assistance

620
00:32:09,361 --> 00:32:13,360
programs throughout New York City.

621
00:32:13,361 --> 00:32:17,360
The Food Bank for New York City is the largest food bank in the

622
00:32:17,361 --> 00:32:20,500
the largest food bank in the United States.

623
00:32:20,501 --> 00:32:24,000
It's part of a network of more than 200 food banks that make up

624
00:32:24,001 --> 00:32:27,200
the nation's leading domestic hunger relief charity,

625
00:32:27,201 --> 00:32:32,600
called Feeding America. In New York City alone,

626
00:32:32,601 --> 00:32:36,420
more than 1.3 million residents are at risk of hunger

627
00:32:36,421 --> 00:32:41,020
and rely on facilities like this to feed their families every day.

628
00:32:41,021 --> 00:32:44,160
In the food bank's effort to feed them, more than 50 million

629
00:32:44,161 --> 00:32:48,600
flows in and out of this warehouse each year.

630
00:32:48,700 --> 00:32:50,960
Our food comes from a variety of sources.

631
00:32:51,060 --> 00:32:53,800
About 65% is from government sources.

632
00:32:53,900 --> 00:32:57,900
35% of the food is donated from really large manufacturers.

633
00:32:58,000 --> 00:33:02,220
Donated food will be coming from your Tropicana, General Mills,

634
00:33:02,320 --> 00:33:06,220
General Foods, Kraft, Conagra.

635
00:33:06,320 --> 00:33:08,220
To keep track of so much product,

636
00:33:08,320 --> 00:33:10,920
the food bank has turned to barcode technology,

637
00:33:11,020 --> 00:33:13,520
similar to his supermarkets.

638
00:33:13,521 --> 00:33:15,760
We have selectors who have scanners.

639
00:33:15,860 --> 00:33:20,320
It tells the selector where he can go in the warehouse,

640
00:33:20,420 --> 00:33:24,160
how many cases he has to select, the order of the selection.

641
00:33:24,260 --> 00:33:27,000
Usually you pick from the heaviest to the lightest.

642
00:33:27,100 --> 00:33:29,440
So we'll do all of that for him.

643
00:33:29,540 --> 00:33:31,540
Four.

644
00:33:31,640 --> 00:33:33,400
With the help of the barcoding system,

645
00:33:33,500 --> 00:33:35,240
the warehouse distributes enough food

646
00:33:35,340 --> 00:33:38,240
to prepare 330,000 meals per day.

647
00:33:38,241 --> 00:33:42,240
per day.

648
00:33:42,340 --> 00:33:44,840
Some of those meals are even prepared by the food bank

649
00:33:44,940 --> 00:33:48,880
at their community kitchen in nearby Harlem.

650
00:33:48,980 --> 00:33:53,080
The soup kitchen serves 2,500 meals each week.

651
00:33:53,180 --> 00:33:54,720
I've worked with other organizations

652
00:33:54,820 --> 00:33:57,900
where I've had to cook 1,500 meals a day.

653
00:33:58,000 --> 00:33:59,200
Here's no different.

654
00:33:59,300 --> 00:34:00,800
Doing the same thing, I want the people

655
00:34:00,900 --> 00:34:03,740
to get the same quality, the same type of food

656
00:34:03,840 --> 00:34:04,840
that other people get.

657
00:34:04,940 --> 00:34:06,000
They're entitled to it.

658
00:34:06,100 --> 00:34:07,900
They can't go to a restaurant, but hey,

659
00:34:07,901 --> 00:34:10,400
Hey, I can bring the restaurant to them.

660
00:34:10,401 --> 00:34:13,140
Tonight's meal feeding 700 people

661
00:34:13,141 --> 00:34:15,860
consists of 50 pounds of Caesar salad,

662
00:34:15,861 --> 00:34:20,440
70 loaves of fresh bread, and 120 gallons of chicken noodle

663
00:34:20,441 --> 00:34:23,240
soup.

664
00:34:23,241 --> 00:34:27,320
The community kitchen also houses a food pantry downstairs,

665
00:34:27,321 --> 00:34:29,240
where qualifying low-income New Yorkers

666
00:34:29,241 --> 00:34:34,440
can obtain fresh produce and packaged foods free of charge.

667
00:34:34,441 --> 00:34:36,740
Each week, the mini supermarket provides food

668
00:34:36,741 --> 00:34:40,640
for more than 13,500 meals.

669
00:34:40,740 --> 00:34:44,240
It helps me a lot, you know, because what the prices

670
00:34:44,340 --> 00:34:47,380
is of today, you know, and what little bit that I need,

671
00:34:47,480 --> 00:34:48,580
it helps me out a lot.

672
00:34:51,580 --> 00:34:54,120
The logistics of feeding a metropolis like New York

673
00:34:54,220 --> 00:34:56,800
may be daunting.

674
00:34:56,900 --> 00:35:00,500
But what about the task of feeding the world?

675
00:35:00,600 --> 00:35:03,400
It's a challenge that the United Nations World Food Program

676
00:35:03,401 --> 00:35:06,840
has tried to meet for nearly half a century.

677
00:35:06,940 --> 00:35:09,800
The World Food Program is the largest humanitarian organization

678
00:35:09,900 --> 00:35:12,740
in the world. We handle a colossal amount of food.

679
00:35:12,840 --> 00:35:17,140
Last year, we sent around about 3.3 million metric tons of food.

680
00:35:17,240 --> 00:35:21,160
It's basically we fed 90 million people in the world

681
00:35:21,260 --> 00:35:23,160
in more than 80 countries.

682
00:35:23,260 --> 00:35:25,360
According to the World Food Program,

683
00:35:25,460 --> 00:35:29,460
more than 923 million people, one out of nearly seven people

684
00:35:29,560 --> 00:35:32,460
on Earth, are chronically hungry.

685
00:35:32,461 --> 00:35:34,700
That makes hunger and malnutrition the number one

686
00:35:34,800 --> 00:35:35,800
health risk worldwide.

687
00:35:39,400 --> 00:35:42,300
About 30% of what the World Food Program ships out

688
00:35:42,400 --> 00:35:45,700
to fight the problem passes through terminals in and around

689
00:35:45,800 --> 00:35:48,300
Norfolk, Virginia.

690
00:35:48,400 --> 00:35:50,620
You can see this rail car here.

691
00:35:50,720 --> 00:35:56,460
This is filled with about 2,500 metric tons of CSB.

692
00:35:56,560 --> 00:35:57,820
That's corn-soya blend.

693
00:35:57,920 --> 00:36:00,820
This is a high-impact, highly nutritional food.

694
00:36:00,821 --> 00:36:05,020
It's one of approximately 36 cars full of food that's

695
00:36:05,120 --> 00:36:08,160
going to the World Food Program in Kenya.

696
00:36:08,260 --> 00:36:12,160
And that's enough to feed a staggering 4.5 million people

697
00:36:12,260 --> 00:36:12,960
for a day.

698
00:36:15,660 --> 00:36:17,840
The loaded containers travel six miles

699
00:36:17,940 --> 00:36:20,680
to an export facility, where the food is transferred

700
00:36:20,780 --> 00:36:21,480
to cargo ships.

701
00:36:24,280 --> 00:36:26,880
At any one point in the day, we have 100 ships

702
00:36:26,881 --> 00:36:31,080
on the high seas, 50 airplanes in the air,

703
00:36:31,180 --> 00:36:34,080
and 10,000 trucks delivering food.

704
00:36:34,180 --> 00:36:37,920
And at the other end, people use any kind of locomotion

705
00:36:38,020 --> 00:36:42,000
you can imagine, from yaks to elephants to donkeys,

706
00:36:42,100 --> 00:36:46,840
to get the food right down to the household level.

707
00:36:46,940 --> 00:36:48,940
Food destined for some of the most dangerous

708
00:36:49,040 --> 00:36:53,580
and isolated areas must make its way there via airdrop.

709
00:36:53,680 --> 00:36:55,080
The experts have worked out systems

710
00:36:55,081 --> 00:36:57,580
to make sure the drops happen safely.

711
00:36:57,680 --> 00:37:01,880
You can imagine metric tons of food hurtling from the sky.

712
00:37:01,980 --> 00:37:02,980
It can kill people.

713
00:37:03,080 --> 00:37:07,720
So the huge cargo plane has to direct the cargo

714
00:37:07,820 --> 00:37:11,660
at just the right moment using the latest GPS technology

715
00:37:11,760 --> 00:37:14,800
to pinpoint it so that the food is launched,

716
00:37:14,900 --> 00:37:19,240
airborne, to land within a fairly contained space that's

717
00:37:19,340 --> 00:37:24,080
marked on the ground at a high rate of speed.

718
00:37:24,081 --> 00:37:26,920
Delivering food from the sky takes the utmost planning

719
00:37:27,020 --> 00:37:29,120
and precision.

720
00:37:29,220 --> 00:37:32,520
But what about creating hundreds of thousands of meals served

721
00:37:32,620 --> 00:37:35,120
in the sky each day?

722
00:37:35,220 --> 00:37:37,820
There are a lot of people getting more than just peanuts

723
00:37:37,920 --> 00:37:39,220
at 35,000 feet.

724
00:37:42,340 --> 00:37:45,540
In 2008, the United States was the leading donor to the World

725
00:37:45,640 --> 00:37:51,180
Food Program, with more than $2 billion of food contributions.

726
00:37:51,181 --> 00:37:55,020
Saudi Arabia ranked second with more than $500 million.

727
00:38:01,120 --> 00:38:03,520
These chefs in Inwood, New York,

728
00:38:03,620 --> 00:38:06,920
are whipping up a mega meal for as many as 10,000 people.

729
00:38:07,020 --> 00:38:09,840
That includes everything from lobster tail

730
00:38:09,940 --> 00:38:13,140
to filet mignon to chicken breast.

731
00:38:13,240 --> 00:38:16,780
Expectations for this food are literally sky high,

732
00:38:16,880 --> 00:38:19,880
because everything you see here will be served in flight

733
00:38:19,881 --> 00:38:23,880
at 35,000 feet. Even though the meal on a typical

734
00:38:23,980 --> 00:38:28,880
U.S. domestic flight these days is just a simple snack, on most

735
00:38:28,980 --> 00:38:33,820
international flights you can still get the real deal.

736
00:38:33,920 --> 00:38:38,240
And this Gate Gourmet JFK Airport flight kitchen is just

737
00:38:38,340 --> 00:38:41,740
one of about 100 locations around the globe that in total

738
00:38:41,840 --> 00:38:46,140
prepare more than 200 million airline meals each year.

739
00:38:46,141 --> 00:38:49,880
This facility does about 50,000 meals a week.

740
00:38:49,980 --> 00:38:53,240
Our customer base here runs from Middle Eastern Airlines

741
00:38:53,340 --> 00:38:56,020
to European Airlines, Latin American Airlines,

742
00:38:56,120 --> 00:38:58,520
and domestic US airlines as well.

743
00:38:58,620 --> 00:39:01,580
We operate 24 hours a day, seven days a week,

744
00:39:01,680 --> 00:39:03,860
365 days a year.

745
00:39:03,960 --> 00:39:04,960
We never close.

746
00:39:07,220 --> 00:39:09,860
In-flight catering is an $18 billion

747
00:39:09,960 --> 00:39:14,700
industry worldwide with a distinctly international flavor.

748
00:39:14,701 --> 00:39:16,400
We do authentic Thai food.

749
00:39:16,500 --> 00:39:18,000
We do authentic Chinese food.

750
00:39:18,100 --> 00:39:20,700
And every meal that we design and put on a plane

751
00:39:20,800 --> 00:39:24,040
is geared specifically to that region.

752
00:39:24,140 --> 00:39:26,380
The aircraft that we handle include everything

753
00:39:26,480 --> 00:39:28,940
from the largest, the Airbus A380,

754
00:39:29,040 --> 00:39:31,620
all the way down to the smallest Super 80s.

755
00:39:31,720 --> 00:39:34,380
We're taking the largest example, the A380.

756
00:39:34,480 --> 00:39:36,120
It's flying 16 hours.

757
00:39:36,220 --> 00:39:39,360
There's going to be 2,000 meals put on that flight.

758
00:39:39,460 --> 00:39:44,000
Imagine, 2,000 meals all delivered at the same time.

759
00:39:44,001 --> 00:39:46,240
What restaurant does that?

760
00:39:46,340 --> 00:39:49,640
But before any in-flight mega-meal makes the menu,

761
00:39:49,740 --> 00:39:53,340
the chefs must test their recipes in smaller batches,

762
00:39:53,440 --> 00:39:55,540
like this Kung Pao chicken made to feed 10.

763
00:40:02,360 --> 00:40:05,560
This is ready to go.

764
00:40:05,660 --> 00:40:07,460
Once this dish is up to snuff,

765
00:40:07,560 --> 00:40:10,900
the chef can supersize it to more than 300 servings per batch.

766
00:40:14,000 --> 00:40:19,000
And then it's business as usual in the kitchen

767
00:40:19,100 --> 00:40:22,600
with the addition of one really cool piece of equipment.

768
00:40:22,700 --> 00:40:24,940
One thing that makes in-flight totally unique

769
00:40:25,040 --> 00:40:26,940
is blast chillers.

770
00:40:27,040 --> 00:40:29,340
We're required by the FDA to bring our food

771
00:40:29,440 --> 00:40:34,920
from whatever degree it is, 170, 190 degrees to 45 degrees.

772
00:40:35,020 --> 00:40:36,520
And we use the blast chillers.

773
00:40:36,620 --> 00:40:39,500
Notice that the food is all packed in two-inch pans.

774
00:40:39,600 --> 00:40:42,800
There's a complete revolution of cold air that

775
00:40:42,801 --> 00:40:44,540
that gets in between each pan.

776
00:40:44,640 --> 00:40:48,740
This allows the food to get chilled in less than 30 minutes.

777
00:40:48,840 --> 00:40:51,280
Airline meals may be prepared as much as 10 hours

778
00:40:51,380 --> 00:40:53,340
before being devoured.

779
00:40:53,440 --> 00:40:56,340
So the blast chiller ensures no bacteria forms in the food.

780
00:40:59,480 --> 00:41:00,760
All the food has been prepared.

781
00:41:00,860 --> 00:41:01,760
It's been chilled.

782
00:41:01,860 --> 00:41:03,280
The food production employee will bring it

783
00:41:03,380 --> 00:41:05,360
into this big refrigerator unit.

784
00:41:05,460 --> 00:41:07,520
So notice the temperature is always constant.

785
00:41:07,620 --> 00:41:09,820
It's always 40, 45 degrees.

786
00:41:09,920 --> 00:41:12,660
The employee has what we call a specification of the food.

787
00:41:12,661 --> 00:41:15,300
item to tell them exactly what goes into it,

788
00:41:15,400 --> 00:41:17,200
and then they'll go ahead and start packing.

789
00:41:17,300 --> 00:41:20,860
We usually do no more than 20 or 30 meals at a time

790
00:41:20,960 --> 00:41:23,740
because we don't want the food exposed any longer than it has

791
00:41:23,840 --> 00:41:26,200
to be.

792
00:41:26,300 --> 00:41:28,340
Each day, more than 40 employees

793
00:41:28,440 --> 00:41:30,540
brave the cold room to dish out the meals that

794
00:41:30,640 --> 00:41:34,420
will be reheated and served in flight.

795
00:41:34,520 --> 00:41:36,320
But it's still up to the airline cooks here

796
00:41:36,420 --> 00:41:39,260
to make sure the meal is tasty.

797
00:41:39,360 --> 00:41:41,860
About 15 years ago, a study was done,

798
00:41:41,861 --> 00:41:46,060
And it was found out that 18% to 20% of the flavor profile

799
00:41:46,160 --> 00:41:49,900
was lost at 30,000 feet, mostly blamed on pressurization

800
00:41:50,000 --> 00:41:51,500
from the cabin.

801
00:41:51,600 --> 00:41:55,100
So what we do in in-flight is that means that we have to find

802
00:41:55,200 --> 00:41:58,100
techniques to make the food more flavorful.

803
00:41:58,200 --> 00:42:00,320
For example, unlike a restaurant where something

804
00:42:00,420 --> 00:42:02,820
can be marinated for two hours, we'll go ahead and we'll

805
00:42:02,920 --> 00:42:07,020
marinate something for six or eight hours.

806
00:42:07,120 --> 00:42:09,460
One trick the gate gourmet chefs have learned

807
00:42:09,461 --> 00:42:11,960
is how to help ensure that what they cook here

808
00:42:11,961 --> 00:42:14,300
won't be overcooked during reheating on the plane,

809
00:42:15,300 --> 00:42:17,560
like this steak for the first-class passengers.

810
00:42:19,140 --> 00:42:21,200
We always prepare the steak rare

811
00:42:21,201 --> 00:42:23,500
because this way the flight attendant

812
00:42:23,501 --> 00:42:25,540
can leave it in the oven an extra five minutes

813
00:42:25,541 --> 00:42:26,860
and get a medium steak,

814
00:42:26,960 --> 00:42:28,740
or pull it out when they're supposed to

815
00:42:28,741 --> 00:42:29,700
and get a medium-rare steak.

816
00:42:30,680 --> 00:42:33,180
Even vegetables get special treatment in this kitchen.

817
00:42:34,340 --> 00:42:36,000
Whether the vegetables are served hot

818
00:42:36,001 --> 00:42:38,240
or they're served cold, they get blanched.

819
00:42:38,241 --> 00:42:41,140
Chef Yosha here has some boiling water over here.

820
00:42:41,240 --> 00:42:43,740
He's going to add some salt to it.

821
00:42:43,840 --> 00:42:46,480
He's going to add the vegetables to it.

822
00:42:46,580 --> 00:42:48,680
And notice how quickly he blanches it.

823
00:42:51,980 --> 00:42:53,080
Once the vegetables are cooked, they

824
00:42:53,180 --> 00:42:55,400
get dropped into an ice bath.

825
00:42:55,500 --> 00:42:57,100
This is called shocking the vegetables.

826
00:42:57,200 --> 00:42:59,700
And what it does is it stops the cooking process

827
00:42:59,800 --> 00:43:01,600
and kind of guarantees that the vegetables are not

828
00:43:01,700 --> 00:43:04,000
going to be overcooked.

829
00:43:04,100 --> 00:43:06,340
But preparing a meal for airline passengers

830
00:43:06,341 --> 00:43:10,180
wasn't always such an exact science.

831
00:43:10,280 --> 00:43:12,180
Back in the 30s, the first in-flight caterer

832
00:43:12,280 --> 00:43:13,380
was the flight attendant.

833
00:43:13,480 --> 00:43:15,140
They would stop off, they would pick up donuts,

834
00:43:15,240 --> 00:43:16,880
they would pick up thermoses of coffee,

835
00:43:16,980 --> 00:43:18,380
and that was the meal service.

836
00:43:18,480 --> 00:43:20,680
The 60s is when things really started rolling.

837
00:43:20,780 --> 00:43:24,500
That's when the 707s got involved, and they had galleys.

838
00:43:24,600 --> 00:43:26,220
So we were able to bulk board food,

839
00:43:26,320 --> 00:43:28,300
and the flight attendant was able to heat it up.

840
00:43:28,400 --> 00:43:30,860
Now you not only had a galley to heat food up,

841
00:43:30,960 --> 00:43:33,700
but you had flight attendants rolling down carts

842
00:43:33,800 --> 00:43:36,300
and carving prime rib and cutting up wood

843
00:43:36,340 --> 00:43:41,080
lobster for the first-class passengers.

844
00:43:41,180 --> 00:43:43,180
Flight attendants may not carve rib roasts

845
00:43:43,280 --> 00:43:44,980
in the aisles anymore.

846
00:43:45,080 --> 00:43:47,780
But thanks to the experts at Gate Gourmet,

847
00:43:47,880 --> 00:43:49,780
airline passengers can still look forward

848
00:43:49,880 --> 00:43:51,380
to a world-class mega meal.

849
00:43:54,600 --> 00:43:58,500
Whether hunger strikes a crowd in flight or out at sea,

850
00:43:58,600 --> 00:44:01,200
during a ball game, or at the slots,

851
00:44:01,300 --> 00:44:04,640
the experts have the recipe for satisfying it.

852
00:44:04,641 --> 00:44:09,120
When it comes to Mega Meals, there's no such thing as too many cooks in the kitchen.

